The '''Hotel Senador''' ({{lang|ca|Hotel Senador}}) is a five-star hotel occupying floors 18 through 35 of the [[International Finance Building]] at 2 [[Embarcador CR]], [[Sant Cristòfor]], [[Balboa]]. The hotel was originally established in 1858 as a freestanding building on the same site, and served as a cultural and intellectual centre of Sant Cristòfor for nearly a century before its destruction during the [[Great Adonian War]]. The current hotel was rebuilt in 1962 as part of the International Finance Building skyscraper, erected over the original location. The hotel's 32nd floor houses [[Magpalibog]], a Dagit fine-dining restaurant ranked Nº18 on [[Abyala's 50 Best Restaurants]].

== History ==

=== Original hotel (1858–1947) ===

The Hotel Senador was built in 1858 during the early industrial expansion of Sant Cristòfor, when the city's commercial class was beginning to establish permanent institutions beyond the colonial-era infrastructure. The building was a four-story stone structure in the Volisanian colonial style, with a central courtyard, arched galleries, and a terracotta roof. It was initially a modest establishment catering to merchants and government officials, but its position on the [[Embarcador CR]] — the principal harbourfront promenade — placed it at the centre of the city's public life.

[[File:Hotel Senador original.jpg|thumb|The original Hotel Senador, c. 1920]]

=== Zong era intellectual hub ===

During the [[Zong Balboa]] period (1899–1950), the Hotel Senador became the most important gathering place for the intellectual and artistic community of Sant Cristòfor. The hotel's ground-floor café — the [[Cafè del Senador]] — served as an informal salon where Zong, Balboan, and Sinchewese writers, journalists, artists, and political figures met regularly. The café was known for its permissive atmosphere: the Zong colonial administration maintained a light hand over the hotel, which was owned by a Zong family that valued its reputation as a neutral space, and the resulting tolerance attracted figures who might otherwise have been subject to surveillance or censorship.

The Zong-era intellectual scene at the Hotel Senador was defined by several overlapping circles. The most prominent was the [[Cercle del Port]] ("Harbour Circle"), a group of Zong-Balboan and Sinchewese writers who gathered at the Cafè del Senador every Saturday evening to read poetry, debate politics, and discuss the cultural tensions of the occupation period. The Cercle's membership included the Zong-Balboan poet [[Lîm Chheng-hong]], whose 1928 collection {{lang|zh|海邊的聲}} ("Voices from the Shore") was written largely in the café; the Sinchewese journalist [[Nor Pham]], who published the underground newspaper [[L'Estret]] from a print shop two blocks from the hotel; and the Balboan Creole novelist [[Elena Vargas]], whose novel [[The Walls]] (1954) was set in the hotel during the siege of Sant Cristòfor.

A second circle was the [[Grup Modernista]], a collective of visual artists and architects who used the hotel's second-floor gallery as an exhibition space from 1915 until the war. The Grup Modernista introduced modernist aesthetics to Balboan art and produced the first significant body of work that attempted to synthesize Zong, Volisanian, and Balboan visual traditions. Their exhibitions at the Hotel Senador were among the few public events in Zong Balboa where Zong, Balboan, and Sinchewese communities socialised as equals.

The hotel also hosted political discussions that shaped the post-war settlement. During the siege of Sant Cristòfor in the [[Great Adonian War]], the hotel's basement served as a meeting point for the Balboan resistance leadership, and several of the negotiations that preceded the dissolution of Zong Balboa in 1950 were conducted in the hotel's private rooms.

=== Destruction in the Great Adonian War ===

The original Hotel Senador was destroyed in 1944 during the bombing of Sant Cristòfor by Volisanian forces. The stone structure survived the initial bombardment but was gutted by fire; the courtyard, galleries, and the Cafè del Senador were entirely lost. The ruins stood on the Embarcador site for over a decade after the war.

=== Rebuilding (1962) ===

In 1962, the site was incorporated into the construction of the [[International Finance Building]], a 64-story skyscraper that became the centrepiece of Sant Cristòfor's post-war financial district. The Hotel Senador was rebuilt within the new structure, occupying floors 18 through 35. The rebuilt hotel bore no architectural resemblance to the original — it was a modern high-rise hotel with floor-to-ceiling windows, air conditioning, and the amenities expected of a 1960s luxury establishment — but the name, the location, and the institutional memory of the original were preserved.

The decision to rebuild the hotel within an office skyscraper rather than as a freestanding building was controversial. Critics argued that the International Finance Building represented the erasure of the hotel's cultural heritage in favour of commercial development, and that the new Hotel Senador was a hotel in name only. Supporters countered that the rebuilding preserved the hotel's presence on the Embarcador and that the alternative — leaving the site vacant or selling it to a purely commercial developer — would have been a greater loss.

== Modern hotel ==

The Hotel Senador operates as a five-star hotel with 312 rooms and suites across eighteen floors of the International Finance Building. The hotel's position on floors 18–35 places it above the commercial and office levels of the building and below the upper office floors, with views of the Bay of Sant Cristòfor and the Shendan harbour.

=== Restaurants ===

The hotel's dining venues include [[Magpalibog]], the Dagit fine-dining restaurant on the 32nd floor, ranked Nº18 on [[Abyala's 50 Best Restaurants]] (2023); the [[Terrassa del Senador]], a Volisanian-Balboan restaurant on the 19th floor with a terrace overlooking the bay; and [[El Mirador]], a cocktail bar on the 35th floor.

=== Legacy ===

The Hotel Senador's cultural significance remains tied to its Zong-era history. The Cafè del Senador, though destroyed in 1944, is commemorated in the hotel's lobby with a small exhibition of photographs, manuscripts, and artifacts from the Cercle del Port and the Grup Modernista. The hotel's management maintains a relationship with the Sant Cristòfor literary and arts community, and the lobby exhibition is a regular stop on the city's cultural walking tours.

